WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.710
- I love variations of this joke

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:05.320
because everyone hates
lawyers until you need one.

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.250
And then, then who you gonna call?

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.005
Hmm, hmm, hmm.

00:00:09.005 --> 00:00:12.310
♪Ghost Busters. ♪

00:00:12.310 --> 00:00:15.430
Meme Review, trademarked of PewDiePie.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:16.410
All rights reserved.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:17.690
All right, let's just get at it.

00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:19.510
Legal memes, 2021 style.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:20.343
Let's do it.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.050
I graduated from Law School six years ago

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:26.270
with $250,000 of student loan debt.

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:27.420
After years of hard work

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.500
and tens of thousands
of dollars of payments,

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.130
I can officially say
now that I owe $315,000.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:34.080
Very, very true.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.457
Law School is extremely expensive.

00:00:35.457 --> 00:00:39.410
$250,000, a quarter of a
million dollars for three years.

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:43.640
Not unusual if you are
making the minimum payments.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.540
Yeah, that amount can
actually increase over time.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.340
Now lawyers do tend to make more than

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:51.800
the national median salary

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.970
but that doesn't mean that
the amounts that they pay

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.810
for law school, aren't huge.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:58.140
I know a lot of people who owe more now

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.600
than they did when they graduated.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.470
And of course, a lot of people
rely on loan forgiveness.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.410
They might work for a
public interest law firm

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:07.190
or they might work for the government,

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:08.960
in the hopes that over time

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.330
they will have their loans
forgiven by the government.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:14.120
Which can happen.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.710
There were talks of scaling that back,

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.810
but man, it's a, it's kind of a crapshoot.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:20.040
You know what if you
leave the government job

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.223
it's it can really work
out poorly sometimes, so.

00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:28.166
♪Toss a coin to your lawyer. ♪

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.220
The first thing I learned in law school

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:33.590
is to check how long something
is before I start reading it.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.320
This is actually really,
really good advice.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.200
Not all cases are created equal.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.050
In law school you get these casebooks,

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:40.970
you are supposed to read these cases,

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.430
but some cases are 20, 30, 50 pages long

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.480
and other cases are condensed

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.370
and they might only be two pages long.

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:49.950
And the amount of information that

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.030
you're probably going to take
from each one of these cases

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:53.350
is about the same.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.610
Everyone has a limited
amount of time in Law School.

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.130
I have a whole course on how
to do well in Law School.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:00.260
And time management is a
huge, huge portion of that.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:03.550
Knowing that you might
have to skim one case,

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.480
as opposed to reading one case in depth

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.320
and maybe knowing ahead of time,

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:08.660
which cases are the important ones

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.410
and which ones are filling
in some background idea.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.090
Super, super important.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.643
This guy gets it for sure.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:18.510
All right keeping with
the Law School theme.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:19.940
Law School has taught
me the valuable skill

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.187
of discussing the readings
without having read the readings

00:02:22.187 --> 00:02:24.080
#LawStudentProblems.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.140
Yeah the other part of that would be

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.090
sometimes you're not able to
get to all of the readings.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:31.400
It takes forever to read these cases

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.480
and you might have literally
hundreds of pages to read,

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:35.680
per night in Law School.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.620
Now there's a whole cottage
industry of supplements

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.020
that help you get through this

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.950
and synthesize everything into smaller,

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:43.770
more manageable pieces.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.610
But sometimes there's
just way too much reading

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.280
and you have to let things slip.

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.040
Of course, you have to worry
about the Socratic method.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.640
So that if you're not prepared in class,

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.000
the professor might call
on you, he might grill you.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.890
And you might not have done the reading

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.290
and that doesn't turn out well,

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:59.710
just, you know see what
happens in Legally Blonde.

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.030
- Now, I assume all of you have read

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.730
pages 1 through 48 and are now well versed

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:06.520
in subject matter jurisdiction.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:09.733
- Which is quasi-realistic, I guess.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.280
Okay, out of the Law School
and into the practice.

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:16.560
When you check in with the clerk

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.760
and try to get priority,
with the sunglass shades.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.270
So here's the background here.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:24.790
Every courthouse has a court clerk,

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:26.320
they're an administrative official

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.000
but they can sort of control your destiny.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.860
There might be a court clerk
for the whole courthouse

00:03:30.860 --> 00:03:33.380
and there might be a court
clerk for the particular judge

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:34.360
that you're in front of.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:38.110
And they control the
calendars of the judges.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:41.260
So sometimes you should probably

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.280
spend some time getting
to know your court clerk.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:44.113
At least being nice.

00:03:44.113 --> 00:03:46.690
There's so many lawyers who
are just really, really mean

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:47.650
to these court clerks.

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:50.190
Thinking that they're,
you know beneath them

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:51.023
to talk to them.

00:03:51.023 --> 00:03:53.090
But in reality a court clerk is the grease

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:54.970
that makes the wheels
of the courthouse work.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:56.410
So, you better be nice

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:59.973
and you know metaphorically
slide into their DMS I guess.

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:04.343
I hate citations why
can't you just trust me?

00:04:04.343 --> 00:04:06.360
(laughs heartily)

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:07.430
Oh my gosh.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:12.340
Having practiced law for, I
don't know, 12 or 13 years now,

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.750
citations are the lifeblood
of any legal brief.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:18.220
You have to cite any proposition of law

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:19.930
that you're saying to the judge,

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:22.310
otherwise court and council will look at

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:24.800
whatever you have just said
with a great deal of suspicion.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.460
No one's going to trust your citations.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.280
And the funny thing is
when we do scripts here

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:29.960
on Legal Eagle, you don't usually see it

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.640
but the scripts have on
average 75 or a 100 citations

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:35.510
because we had to do a lot of research

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:37.030
because we can't get these things wrong.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:38.730
Whether we're putting
something in front of the judge

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.840
or putting something in front
of all the people on YouTube,

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.230
the citations are important.

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:44.280
And although you might
not see the citations

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:45.580
they are definitely there.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:49.890
Unemployment checks,
murder hornets, quarantine,

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:52.070
loan debt, Coronavirus.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:53.640
I'll go to law school.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:54.800
That's fine.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:55.633
That's fine.

00:04:55.633 --> 00:04:56.630
Oh my God.

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.880
Yeah, so shed a tear for the law students

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:03.960
who have been going to law
school in 2020 and 2021,

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:05.070
it's completely different.

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:06.510
It's all virtual.

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:09.280
You are losing out on a
whole lot of the advantages

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:10.430
of law school.

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:11.910
And it's, it's really unfortunate.

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:13.700
And not only is it, you know,

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:16.820
the whole world is sort
of on fire or whatever,

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.630
but yeah to add Law School on top of that

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:21.980
and you don't even get to
interact with people in person.

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:23.250
Now, maybe that's a good thing

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.940
'cause you're not getting
the stress by proxy

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:27.280
of all your fellow law students.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.550
Man, that would be really tough to add on

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.490
all of this stuff with law school stuff.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:34.060
Though on the other hand, you know

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:36.040
you're not going to interact
with a whole lot of people

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:37.280
normally in Law School anyway.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.910
So maybe it's best to get
Law School out of the way

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:41.110
while everyone is cooped up.

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:43.060
And then, you know, you
can leave Law School

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:45.610
and reenter society like
a normal human being.

00:05:45.610 --> 00:05:46.810
Maybe that's not so bad.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.560
Which Supreme Court
Justice flushed the toilet

00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:52.096
on today's conference call.

00:05:52.096 --> 00:05:54.530
Oh man, this was a huge, huge deal.

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:56.480
Someone actually flushed the toilet,

00:05:56.480 --> 00:05:58.820
while an oral argument
with the Supreme Court.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:02.440
Now the Supreme Court famously
doesn't allow video cameras

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.760
inside of the courthouse itself.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.060
So, you get a bunch of these like oil

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:10.540
and pen paintings of people,

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:12.220
while they're presenting an oral argument.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:15.070
But now in the Coronavirus times,

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:16.130
they have had to adapt

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:20.420
and they had to go to a dial
in voice conference system.

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.460
And of course, one of
the first oral arguments

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:25.110
that this was happening,
someone wasn't on mute

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.800
and you could hear a toilet
flushing in the background.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:29.560
- And what the FCC has said is that

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:32.280
when the subject matter of the fall ranges

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:35.470
to topics, then the call is transformed.

00:06:35.470 --> 00:06:38.470
- John Roberts must have been apoplectic

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:41.380
because he cares about how
the court is perceived.

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:43.670
And if there's plumbing in the background

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:44.800
of an oral argument

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:47.680
you can just imagine how
furious he must have been.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:51.763
Not, not great for the, the
image of the Supreme Court.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:54.653
All right.

00:06:54.653 --> 00:06:56.410
What Law School looks
like from Facebook user

00:06:56.410 --> 00:06:57.750
Trust Me, I'm a Lawyer.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:07:00.500
I mean, this is this is actually
what Law School looks like.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.370
This is not a joke.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:05.010
So one of these books is a case book

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:07.280
and then a couple of these
books are supplements.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:08.530
I think there's another case book.

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:12.400
And then there are obviously
tons and tons of highly.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.420
I would say that this
actually under represents,

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.990
how much highlighting and inner lineation

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:19.460
and note-taking you actually take.

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:21.540
And little plastic flags in the book.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:23.843
Law students actually have
to deal with this crap.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:26.160
All right, how they welcome

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.300
newly hired associates to a law firm.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.200
Some of you may die

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:31.610
but it's a sacrifice I'm willing to make.

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:32.970
So here's the thing with big firms

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:34.770
they pay a huge amount of money.

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:38.150
They expect for you to
basically devote your life

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:40.520
to the big firm when you
are a young associate

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:43.360
and they will actually
train you how to be a lawyer

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:46.200
at the highest levels of jurisprudence.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:48.120
I actually think it's
a reasonable trade off.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:51.270
I was in the big firm world for 10 years.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:55.320
And I learned to practice at
the absolute highest levels

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:58.760
of Federal Court, in high
level State Court proceedings.

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:01.820
For a long time, I was completely
devoted to that big firm.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:03.400
And I actually wouldn't
trade that forever.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:06.100
You know the phrase, they don't
pay me enough for this crap.

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:08.800
Well, big firms actually do
pay you enough for that crap.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.440
Whatever that crap is,
you kind of have to do it.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:12.610
And it's a reasonable trade off

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:14.930
because Law School doesn't
really teach you how

00:08:14.930 --> 00:08:16.040
to be a lawyer.

00:08:16.040 --> 00:08:17.200
You have to learn that on the job.

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:18.450
Not for everyone.

00:08:18.450 --> 00:08:19.400
It's hard to get into

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.400
but the trade-off is sometimes worth it.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:25.990
When you start working at your
first job after Law School.

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:28.140
I don't bleep know about bleep.

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:30.370
All right I think we have to
probably bleep that one out.

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:32.860
So as I was just saying, you
graduate from Law School,

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:33.750
you don't know anything.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.400
You don't know bleep about bleep.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:37.730
So true, which is why it's
often a good idea to work

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:38.563
for a big firm

00:08:38.563 --> 00:08:40.760
or another experienced lawyer
who knows what they're doing

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:42.790
'cause when you graduate from Law School

00:08:42.790 --> 00:08:43.990
you don't know anything.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:47.003
All right.

00:08:47.003 --> 00:08:48.020
In keeping with the practice,

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:51.380
me coming up with multiple
good points after the hearing.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:53.520
Oh, with the drag on the cigarette.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:54.560
It happens every time.

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:57.070
well of the time, because
you're at oral argument,

00:08:57.070 --> 00:08:58.390
you're at a hearing

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:00.000
and in the spur of the moment the judge

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:00.960
has some crazy question.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:03.950
You really, really try and
shed this as hard as you can.

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:06.480
You come up with as many
arguments as you can.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:07.550
You talk with your coworkers,

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:08.710
you talk with your friends,

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:11.660
you try and come up with every
argument you possibly can

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:13.380
but inevitably, there's
going to be something

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:14.330
you didn't think of.

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:17.340
And of course the second
that you leave the courthouse

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:18.890
you think of all of these great arguments

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:20.300
that you could have made.

00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:21.320
And you didn't

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:24.433
C'est la vie.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:28.810
- All right, I shall
grant you three wishes.

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:30.690
I wish for a world without lawyers.

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:32.040
Done you have no more wishes.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:33.260
But you said three.

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:34.093
Sue me.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.990
I love variations of this joke

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:40.630
because everyone hates
lawyers until you need one.

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:42.520
And then, then who you gonna call?

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.269
Hmm, hmm, hmm.

00:09:44.269 --> 00:09:47.019
♪Ghost Busters! ♪

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:48.733
- All right.

00:09:48.733 --> 00:09:49.750
The judge throughout our case,

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:51.300
don't worry I brought it back to him.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:53.440
A legal Corgi, ah.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:56.150
Legal Corgis have become a real thing.

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:58.730
I think it was partially
because of George Conway

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:00.630
and his love of Corgis.

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:02.000
And there are a couple
other legal commentators

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.100
that just have Corgis and love them.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:05.890
So they've sort of been appropriated

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.950
as the dog of the legal profession.

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:09.783
This is a good boy.

00:10:09.783 --> 00:10:10.910
He brought his case back.

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:14.970
First, we'll file a
baseless voter fraud claim.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.170
Then it will be 12(b)(6)'d with prejudice.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:19.060
Then we'll get rule 11 sanctions.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:21.790
Then we'll get rule 11 sanctions.

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:23.220
So, this pretty much sums up

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:25.600
all of the elections cases, in general.

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:29.420
A 12(b)(6) is famously a motion
to dismiss in federal court.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:31.090
It is something that you file

00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:33.050
when the claims themselves

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:34.970
don't rise to the level of a legal claim,

